WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.454
Recover one real value first.

00:00:02.454 --> 00:00:05.947
Fix x on the red interval inside the moving contour.

00:00:05.947 --> 00:00:08.159
The Cauchy kernel has its pole at x.

00:00:08.159 --> 00:00:14.211
Turn once counterclockwise around that pole, weighting the kernel by the boundary values of the function.

00:00:14.211 --> 00:00:17.255
Cauchy's formula recovers the value at x.

00:00:17.255 --> 00:00:24.182
Now let x run along the interval, weight these recoveries by the second Legendre polynomial, and integrate.

00:00:24.182 --> 00:00:28.037
Collecting their kernels gives the second-kind kernel Q two.

00:00:28.037 --> 00:00:34.047
With the coefficient normalization, a factor of five remains outside the contour integral.

00:00:34.047 --> 00:00:36.071
Let the tangent turn the product.

00:00:36.071 --> 00:00:38.348
One angle chooses the boundary point.

00:00:38.348 --> 00:00:42.935
As the point turns, the pink tangent gives its direction and speed per radian.

00:00:42.935 --> 00:00:45.489
At the right tip the tangent points straight up.

00:00:45.489 --> 00:00:47.168
At the top it points left.

00:00:47.168 --> 00:00:54.806
Now multiply the function value, the second-kind kernel and this tangent to obtain the violet vector in the output plane.

00:00:54.806 --> 00:00:58.732
Dividing by i turns that vector clockwise by a quarter turn.

00:00:58.732 --> 00:01:07.324
Multiplying its length by five over two pi gives the gold arrow, the rate of change of the accumulated integral per radian.

00:01:07.324 --> 00:01:08.842
Halve the angular step.

00:01:08.842 --> 00:01:13.088
Fix the starting angle at thirty degrees and hold its gold rate fixed.

00:01:13.088 --> 00:01:17.313
Sweep the cyan arc through four tenths of a radian on the inner ellipse.

00:01:17.313 --> 00:01:22.668
Multiplying the starting rate by the angle step gives the gold straight-line prediction.

00:01:22.668 --> 00:01:28.962
The true increment integrates the changing rate all along the arc, giving the mint arrow in the output plane.

00:01:28.962 --> 00:01:31.182
Now halve the angle step to two tenths.

00:01:31.182 --> 00:01:39.012
The predicted arrow halves, and the gap between its tip and the integrated tip falls from about sixteen thousandths to four thousandths.

00:01:39.012 --> 00:01:40.911
Halve the step again to one tenth.

00:01:40.911 --> 00:01:43.003
The gap is now below one thousandth.

00:01:43.003 --> 00:01:48.586
These small directed increments add head to tail to form the full contour integral.

00:01:48.586 --> 00:01:51.015
Two contours add different increments.

00:01:51.015 --> 00:01:55.894
Keep the same function and use two inner radii, one point five four and two.

00:01:55.894 --> 00:01:58.256
Start both running integrals at zero.

00:01:58.256 --> 00:02:01.109
Turn both boundary points through the same angle.

00:02:01.109 --> 00:02:06.777
Their radii differ, so they sample different function values, kernel values and tangents.

00:02:06.777 --> 00:02:11.080
The tips in the output plane trace the accumulated complex integrals.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.610
Stop at a quarter turn.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.523
The two tips are visibly apart.

00:02:14.523 --> 00:02:18.891
A partly traversed contour still remembers which ellipse we chose.

00:02:18.891 --> 00:02:21.341
Both circuits give the same coefficient.

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:24.045
Complete the remaining three quarters of each circuit.

00:02:24.045 --> 00:02:28.284
Watch the cyan and pink traces come together on the real axis.

00:02:28.284 --> 00:02:34.805
Both reach the real integral from our first chapter, approximately zero point zero four nine four two.

00:02:34.805 --> 00:02:36.079
Why do they meet?

00:02:36.079 --> 00:02:41.534
The product of the function and the kernel is holomorphic in the region between the two contours.

00:02:41.534 --> 00:02:45.751
Deforming one contour into the other preserves its complete integral.

00:02:45.751 --> 00:02:50.268
Now we can choose any inner contour to estimate that same coefficient.

